1
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:07,007
Morning, captain.
Agent Lisbon.

2
00:00:07,094 --> 00:00:09,574
Jane, consultant.
What do we have here?

3
00:00:09,661 --> 00:00:11,968
Thanks for stepping in.
Homicide Unit owes you one.

4
00:00:13,143 --> 00:00:14,579
Jane.

5
00:00:14,666 --> 00:00:16,929
- You're that psychic fella.
- Right.

6
00:00:17,017 --> 00:00:19,889
Me? I don't
buy into that ESP crap.

7
00:00:19,976 --> 00:00:22,326
- No offense.
- None taken.

8
00:00:22,413 --> 00:00:24,894
Here we have
Ivor Rassmussen, age 34.

9
00:00:24,981 --> 00:00:27,940
Address, River Park.
No sign of the murder weapon.

10
00:00:28,028 --> 00:00:29,159
Store security was
on the scene pretty fast.

11
00:00:29,246 --> 00:00:30,291
We were able
to detain everybody

12
00:00:30,378 --> 00:00:31,988
who was in
the section at the time.

13
00:00:32,075 --> 00:00:34,425
We're holding them over
in the furniture department.

14
00:00:38,212 --> 00:00:41,389
So, Carnac,
what does Ivor say?

15
00:00:41,476 --> 00:00:45,045
- He tell you who done it?
- Huh.

16
00:00:47,786 --> 00:00:50,224
Leather pants, musky cologne,
phallic jewelry.

17
00:00:50,311 --> 00:00:51,877
A ladies' man.

18
00:00:51,964 --> 00:00:54,358
Went after high-end cougars
with moderate success.

19
00:00:54,445 --> 00:00:57,405
Casual cocaine user.
Plays guitar, not well.

20
00:00:57,492 --> 00:01:00,103
Works in the noncreative end
of a creative business.

21
00:01:00,190 --> 00:01:03,541
Advertising, maybe nothing worth
killing anyone over anyway.

22
00:01:03,628 --> 00:01:05,239
So he died
for romantic reasons.

23
00:01:05,326 --> 00:01:07,328
Where are those suspects
you mentioned?

24
00:01:07,415 --> 00:01:09,373
- The witnesses?
- Whatever.

25
00:01:10,983 --> 00:01:13,073
Take it down a notch.
Take it down three notches.

26
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:14,770
Okay. Under control.

27
00:01:14,857 --> 00:01:16,598
We'll get this done quick
and be on our way.

28
00:01:16,685 --> 00:01:18,643
Hello.
We're from the CBI.

29
00:01:18,730 --> 00:01:21,646
'It may well be that
one of you is the murderer.'

30
00:01:21,733 --> 00:01:25,128
If that's so,
I'm gonna find out who.

31
00:01:30,481 --> 00:01:32,396
These two are innocent.
They can go.

32
00:01:32,483 --> 00:01:35,269
Uh, stick around.
We're gonna need a statement.

33
00:01:35,356 --> 00:01:37,532
Neil.

34
00:01:37,619 --> 00:01:39,186
What's the worst mistake
you've ever made? Quick.

35
00:01:39,273 --> 00:01:40,970
Uh, first marriage.

36
00:01:41,057 --> 00:01:43,233
Good answer.
Why so nervous?

37
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:45,844
I'm not. I just, I..

38
00:01:45,931 --> 00:01:47,498
You. Your name?

39
00:01:47,585 --> 00:01:49,457
- Candice.
- Candice.

40
00:01:49,544 --> 00:01:51,937
I like that name.

41
00:01:52,024 --> 00:01:53,156
Oh.

42
00:01:54,984 --> 00:01:57,378
Here. Thank you.

43
00:01:57,465 --> 00:02:00,250
- Hey!
- It's okay. I'm a consultant.

44
00:02:01,991 --> 00:02:04,124
'Candice, take my advice.
Forgive your mother.'

45
00:02:04,211 --> 00:02:07,257
- Maybe the headaches will stop.
- I love my mother.

46
00:02:07,344 --> 00:02:10,042
Oh, I'm wrong.
Ignore my advice.

47
00:02:10,130 --> 00:02:12,523
- You. Your name.
- Reed.

48
00:02:12,610 --> 00:02:15,439
Excuse me.
This is a joke, right?

49
00:02:15,526 --> 00:02:17,963
A joke?
A man has been killed here.

50
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:19,835
Is that funny to you?

51
00:02:19,922 --> 00:02:21,532
- Are you amused?
- No, but--

52
00:02:21,619 --> 00:02:23,186
Then, sit.

53
00:02:25,449 --> 00:02:27,364
Reed, quick question.

54
00:02:27,451 --> 00:02:29,758
Green Lantern versus Thor.
Who wins?

55
00:02:29,845 --> 00:02:31,934
- Thor.
- Oh, yeah.

56
00:02:32,021 --> 00:02:34,284
- Why'd you kill Rassmussen?
- I didn't.

57
00:02:34,371 --> 00:02:36,765
Alright.

58
00:02:36,852 --> 00:02:39,071
Now, you, Miss Grumpy
And Entitled. What's your story?

59
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:41,987
My name is Mandy Schultz.
And I'll have you know that my--

60
00:02:42,074 --> 00:02:44,468
Let me guess. Your husband
is a very important person.

61
00:02:44,555 --> 00:02:47,297
He is, as it happens.

62
00:02:47,384 --> 00:02:48,690
John's on
the Modesto City Council.

63
00:02:48,777 --> 00:02:50,039
Ah.

64
00:02:50,126 --> 00:02:51,736
And this is
totally unprofessional

65
00:02:51,823 --> 00:02:53,999
and disrespectful.

66
00:02:54,086 --> 00:02:55,740
I'll have my husband take this
up with your superiors.

67
00:02:55,827 --> 00:02:57,307
Mandy, Mandy, Mandy.

68
00:02:57,394 --> 00:02:59,004
The diet pills
are messing with your mind.

69
00:02:59,091 --> 00:03:01,006
This is Sacramento,
the big city.

70
00:03:01,093 --> 00:03:02,356
Your husband means nothing here.

71
00:03:02,443 --> 00:03:04,053
Okay, that's enough.
Agent Lisbon?

72
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:06,098
- Give him another moment.
- We don't need a moment.

73
00:03:06,186 --> 00:03:07,883
Case is all but closed.

74
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:10,102
Mandy here is gonna lead us
right to the murder weapon.

75
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:11,408
- What?
- Take my hands.

76
00:03:11,495 --> 00:03:13,454
I need physical contact
to read your mind.

77
00:03:13,541 --> 00:03:15,456
- You're nuts.
- Prove it.

78
00:03:15,543 --> 00:03:17,153
If I'm wrong, if we
can't find the murder weapon

79
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:19,634
then I look like an idiot.

80
00:03:23,072 --> 00:03:24,552
Yes, you will.

81
00:03:26,423 --> 00:03:28,817
- What are you--
- Don't speak.

82
00:03:43,310 --> 00:03:45,703
[instrumental music]

83
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:21,043
Voila!

84
00:04:21,130 --> 00:04:22,653
'The murder weapon.'

85
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:25,830
This is crazy.
I had no idea that was there.

86
00:04:25,917 --> 00:04:28,006
How could you direct us right to
it if you didn't put it there?

87
00:04:28,093 --> 00:04:31,053
I didn't.
I-I don't know.

88
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:33,142
But I-I didn't kill that man.
I-I didn't!

89
00:04:33,229 --> 00:04:34,535
She's right. She has nothing
to do with the murder.

90
00:04:34,622 --> 00:04:35,579
What?

91
00:04:35,666 --> 00:04:37,015
I just used her as a decoy

92
00:04:37,102 --> 00:04:38,495
to lull the real killer
into giving me

93
00:04:38,582 --> 00:04:40,628
clean directional cues.

94
00:04:40,715 --> 00:04:42,630
Oh, come on. What are
you talking about now?

95
00:04:42,717 --> 00:04:45,067
Whatever way he didn't want us
to go is the way that we went.

96
00:04:45,154 --> 00:04:46,111
- Therealkiller.
- Yeah.

97
00:04:46,198 --> 00:04:47,939
You wanna guess who it is?

98
00:04:48,026 --> 00:04:49,332
Come on, Jane.

99
00:04:51,465 --> 00:04:54,206
- Neil.
- Hey! Whoa!

100
00:04:54,294 --> 00:04:56,121
Excuse me.
No, I...wasn't.

101
00:04:56,208 --> 00:04:58,210
- I-I don't even know that man.
- Oh, is that right?

102
00:04:58,298 --> 00:04:59,603
I'm guessing that
he was having an affair

103
00:04:59,690 --> 00:05:01,213
with your current wife.

104
00:05:01,301 --> 00:05:03,172
You found out and you
forgave her like a sucker.

105
00:05:03,259 --> 00:05:05,653
He saw you here,
probably smirked

106
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:10,571
and said something very clever,
made you feel...small.

107
00:05:10,658 --> 00:05:13,051
- Grab him!
- Humiliated you!

108
00:05:17,055 --> 00:05:18,840
Get him! Get him!

109
00:05:18,927 --> 00:05:21,886
Stop! Stop! Uhh!

110
00:05:21,973 --> 00:05:23,671
- Nice work.
- What?

111
00:05:23,758 --> 00:05:25,455
- Case is closed, isn't it?
- 'Hey! Put it down!'

112
00:05:25,542 --> 00:05:26,500
[gunshot]

113
00:05:26,587 --> 00:05:27,979
[woman screams]

114
00:05:28,066 --> 00:05:29,416
[male #1]
'I said drop it now!'

115
00:05:29,503 --> 00:05:30,634
[gunshots]

116
00:05:32,767 --> 00:05:35,335
[theme music]

117
00:05:49,044 --> 00:05:52,700
A dead suspect, quarter million
dollars worth of damages

118
00:05:52,787 --> 00:05:55,703
formal complaints
of misconduct from the SPD

119
00:05:55,790 --> 00:05:57,182
and the Modesto City Council.

120
00:05:57,269 --> 00:05:59,054
Those damages were way padded.

121
00:05:59,141 --> 00:06:01,709
All due respect, Virgil,
I was trying to close the case.

122
00:06:01,796 --> 00:06:03,406
It was my feeling that Red John

123
00:06:03,493 --> 00:06:04,755
should be our priority
given the dozen fresh leads--

124
00:06:04,842 --> 00:06:07,845
Stop. Stop!

125
00:06:07,932 --> 00:06:10,457
That's the nub of the
problem right there, Red John.

126
00:06:10,544 --> 00:06:13,982
Ever since the Tanner incident,
you've been runnin' way too hot.

127
00:06:14,069 --> 00:06:15,810
I fear a terminal screw-up
is coming.

128
00:06:15,897 --> 00:06:17,899
That's not gonna happen.

129
00:06:17,986 --> 00:06:20,858
- I can handle him.
- Canyou?

130
00:06:20,945 --> 00:06:23,383
Maybe since he saved your life,
you're going easy on him.

131
00:06:23,470 --> 00:06:26,690
Maybe you feel compelled
to cut him some slack.

132
00:06:26,777 --> 00:06:28,126
- Mm.
- No, sir.

133
00:06:28,213 --> 00:06:30,390
That is
absolutely not the case.

134
00:06:30,477 --> 00:06:32,870
Hey, Sam, get in here.

135
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,181
- Lisbon.
- Bosco.

136
00:06:39,268 --> 00:06:42,010
Agent Bosco and his unit are
taking over the Red John case.

137
00:06:42,097 --> 00:06:43,925
What? No, no, no.

138
00:06:44,012 --> 00:06:45,579
You have always been
too close to the case

139
00:06:45,666 --> 00:06:48,364
and now both of you
are waytoo close.

140
00:06:48,451 --> 00:06:50,540
- We need to make a change.
- Can we talk about this?

141
00:06:50,627 --> 00:06:53,151
No. You've worked
for Agent Bosco.

142
00:06:53,238 --> 00:06:55,197
'You know that he will do
the job right.'

143
00:06:55,284 --> 00:06:57,068
You need to let my team
finish what they started.

144
00:06:57,155 --> 00:06:58,940
Teresa, no offense

145
00:06:59,027 --> 00:07:01,856
but you guys aren't even close
to catching this guy.

146
00:07:01,943 --> 00:07:04,728
Fresh set of eyes
gotta be a good idea.

147
00:07:04,815 --> 00:07:07,339
What do you know
about the case?

148
00:07:07,427 --> 00:07:09,864
Not much.
He's a serial killer.

149
00:07:09,951 --> 00:07:12,693
Fifteen victims,
including your wife and child.

150
00:07:12,780 --> 00:07:15,870
I can imagine your pain.

151
00:07:15,957 --> 00:07:17,872
Maybe that pain
is clouding your judgment.

152
00:07:22,485 --> 00:07:23,617
Wow.

153
00:07:30,841 --> 00:07:33,714
And me, Sam?
What's clouding myjudgment?

154
00:07:33,801 --> 00:07:36,194
Good question.
You tell me.

155
00:07:49,904 --> 00:07:52,080
What happened?

156
00:07:52,167 --> 00:07:53,560
- We're off Red John.
- What? Forever?

157
00:07:53,647 --> 00:07:55,344
- He can't do that.
- That's not right.

158
00:07:55,431 --> 00:07:57,172
Quiet.

159
00:07:57,259 --> 00:07:58,608
Look, this is my fault

160
00:07:58,695 --> 00:08:00,828
but it's a wake-up call
for all of us.

161
00:08:00,915 --> 00:08:02,830
We've grown slack
and unprofessional.

162
00:08:02,917 --> 00:08:05,746
Look, we need to earn back
the trust that we've lost.

163
00:08:05,833 --> 00:08:08,313
From now on,
we work 100% straight.

164
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:10,751
No more shortcuts. No more ga..

165
00:08:12,448 --> 00:08:15,233
What are you doing?

166
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:16,844
You're leaving?

167
00:08:20,151 --> 00:08:21,544
Well, frankly,
if I can't use this job

168
00:08:21,631 --> 00:08:23,372
to seek some kind of
personal revenge

169
00:08:23,459 --> 00:08:27,071
then, uh, there's not
a whole lot hereforme.

170
00:08:29,596 --> 00:08:31,641
I-I..

171
00:08:31,728 --> 00:08:33,251
I don't make anything better.

172
00:08:33,338 --> 00:08:35,210
I can't bring
dead people back to life.

173
00:08:35,297 --> 00:08:36,864
What good do we do?

174
00:08:36,951 --> 00:08:41,172
We drive around California
visiting unhappy people.

175
00:08:41,259 --> 00:08:43,218
We're fighting
evil and injustice.

176
00:08:43,305 --> 00:08:46,047
And how's that going?
Any progress lately?

177
00:08:46,134 --> 00:08:48,092
We put bad guys away
where they can't hurt people.

178
00:08:48,179 --> 00:08:49,485
That's good enough.

179
00:08:49,572 --> 00:08:51,443
I was doing more good
as a psychic.

180
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:53,968
I was giving people hope,
at least.

181
00:08:54,055 --> 00:08:56,448
False hope, but hope anyway.

182
00:08:56,536 --> 00:08:58,233
You're right. Best you leave.

183
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:00,714
That way you can go back
to being a full-time fraud

184
00:09:00,801 --> 00:09:03,151
and we can continue
our useless jobs in peace.

185
00:09:03,238 --> 00:09:04,587
Well, that's actually not
what I said.

186
00:09:04,674 --> 00:09:06,763
- Yes, it was.
- We're up. Twenty Palms.

187
00:09:06,850 --> 00:09:08,460
Let's go.
You come, too, Van Pelt.

188
00:09:08,548 --> 00:09:10,767
We'll take two vehicles
in case we need to split up.

189
00:09:19,515 --> 00:09:21,865
[Cho]
I'll see you
when you get there.

190
00:09:31,701 --> 00:09:35,096
Boss, no disrespect,
but I think we need Jane.

191
00:09:35,183 --> 00:09:36,619
We'll manage.

192
00:09:36,706 --> 00:09:38,447
Maybe we don't
close as many cases

193
00:09:38,534 --> 00:09:40,754
but the ones we
doclose will be closed right

194
00:09:40,841 --> 00:09:43,017
by professionals.

195
00:09:43,104 --> 00:09:45,367
[clicking]

196
00:09:45,454 --> 00:09:47,369
Van Pelt,
it's not a jet plane.

197
00:09:47,456 --> 00:09:49,458
Okay, okay, okay.

198
00:09:49,545 --> 00:09:51,025
[siren wails]

199
00:09:51,112 --> 00:09:52,156
S-sorry.

200
00:09:52,243 --> 00:09:53,418
[door opens]

201
00:09:59,773 --> 00:10:02,297
Oh, I'm not gonna
leave you in the lurch.

202
00:10:02,384 --> 00:10:05,779
- I'll do one more case.
- Do up your seat belt.

203
00:10:31,456 --> 00:10:32,719
[indistinct radio chatter]

204
00:10:34,546 --> 00:10:35,896
Van Pelt,
you come with me.

205
00:10:35,983 --> 00:10:37,549
You guys talk
to the first responders.

206
00:10:37,637 --> 00:10:39,551
See what they got.

207
00:10:40,901 --> 00:10:42,380
Hey, the apartment's upstairs.

208
00:10:42,467 --> 00:10:44,774
We have a female Caucasian,
late 30's.

209
00:10:44,861 --> 00:10:46,602
Ran license down already.
It's a fake.

210
00:10:46,689 --> 00:10:48,778
- We're calling her Jane Doe.
- Who found the body?

211
00:10:48,865 --> 00:10:50,998
The building manager noticed
her door was open overnight.

212
00:10:53,391 --> 00:10:55,829
Last one down to the left.

213
00:10:55,916 --> 00:10:57,004
- Thank you.
- Mm-hmm.

214
00:10:57,091 --> 00:10:58,396
[cat mews]

215
00:11:13,629 --> 00:11:14,761
[mews]

216
00:11:31,560 --> 00:11:32,822
[Jane]
'Mm-hmm.'

217
00:11:36,043 --> 00:11:38,219
- What's your take?
- Hit with a stun gun.

218
00:11:38,306 --> 00:11:40,264
Then suffocated
with the cushion.

219
00:11:42,571 --> 00:11:44,878
And they were looking
for something obviously.

220
00:11:44,965 --> 00:11:48,708
- Did they find it, Grace?
- 'Um, it's impossible to tell.'

221
00:11:48,795 --> 00:11:51,798
Well, it seems they looked
everywhere possible. Yes?

222
00:11:51,885 --> 00:11:53,190
Yeah.

223
00:11:53,277 --> 00:11:55,323
So we can assume
that they didn't find it

224
00:11:55,410 --> 00:11:57,281
whatever itis.

225
00:11:57,368 --> 00:11:58,761
How so?

226
00:11:58,848 --> 00:12:00,110
Well, let's say
there's a hundred places

227
00:12:00,197 --> 00:12:01,546
to look for a thing.

228
00:12:01,633 --> 00:12:03,287
How likely is it
that you will find it

229
00:12:03,374 --> 00:12:05,855
in the hundredth place you look?
Not very likely, right?

230
00:12:05,942 --> 00:12:06,900
What else you got?

231
00:12:06,987 --> 00:12:09,337
Um...no drug paraphernalia.

232
00:12:09,424 --> 00:12:13,167
No condoms or other items
suggesting prostitution.

233
00:12:13,254 --> 00:12:15,386
So...maybe a personal crime?

234
00:12:15,473 --> 00:12:18,433
- A husband or lover?
- Bravo, Grace.

235
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:20,783
Wrong, I expect, but very
well reasoned, nonetheless.

236
00:12:28,922 --> 00:12:31,925
Thank you, all.
Appreciate it.

237
00:12:32,012 --> 00:12:34,144
Nothing off the car, but we'll
have the lab boys take a look.

238
00:12:34,231 --> 00:12:35,667
It's still registered
to the previous owner.

239
00:12:35,755 --> 00:12:37,278
Sold it for cash
six months ago.

240
00:12:37,365 --> 00:12:41,369
So basically we know
nothing about her. Nice start.

241
00:12:41,456 --> 00:12:43,153
She's a law-abiding
middle-class housewife

242
00:12:43,240 --> 00:12:44,633
that did something bad

243
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:47,070
something she feels
terribly guilty about.

244
00:12:47,157 --> 00:12:49,290
Guilty enough
to make her run away and hide.

245
00:12:51,379 --> 00:12:52,772
Hey! That's evidence.

246
00:12:52,859 --> 00:12:54,817
- You know the drill.
- Meh.

247
00:12:54,904 --> 00:12:58,865
If you put "Bullfrogs win
high school basketball trophy"

248
00:12:58,952 --> 00:13:00,649
into a search engine

249
00:13:00,736 --> 00:13:03,434
we will find
Jane Doe's home.

250
00:13:03,521 --> 00:13:06,002
- Can I take a look at that?
- Sure.

251
00:13:06,089 --> 00:13:08,483
[Rigsby]
'It's a working truck.
Dad must be in construction.'

252
00:13:08,570 --> 00:13:09,963
Or something like that.

253
00:13:10,050 --> 00:13:12,574
""Bayshore Courier,"
April 2006.

254
00:13:12,661 --> 00:13:14,228
"County finals,
Lady Bullfrogs dominate

255
00:13:14,315 --> 00:13:16,056
injury-plagued Panthers."

256
00:13:16,143 --> 00:13:17,971
If they list the players,
cross-check the surnames

257
00:13:18,058 --> 00:13:19,973
with other articles from the
same newspaper in the last year

258
00:13:20,060 --> 00:13:21,844
using the word disappearance.

259
00:13:21,931 --> 00:13:24,020
If we leave now, we can be in
Bayshore in a couple of hours.

260
00:13:24,107 --> 00:13:26,806
Uh, we need a little more
than Lady Bullfrogs to go on.

261
00:13:29,112 --> 00:13:30,722
[Van Pelt]
'"Bayshore Courier"
of June this year.'

262
00:13:30,810 --> 00:13:34,074
Headline is
"Police no closer to Dunninger."

263
00:13:34,161 --> 00:13:36,380
"Since her disappearance last
month, Bayshore PD have been

264
00:13:36,467 --> 00:13:40,036
"unable to locate 43-year-old
Monica Dunninger, the secretary

265
00:13:40,123 --> 00:13:41,908
'"accused of stealing
nearly a million dollars'

266
00:13:41,995 --> 00:13:43,866
from her employers,
The Jaffe Printing Group."

267
00:13:43,953 --> 00:13:47,739
- Mm.
- Okay. Bayshore it is then.

268
00:13:47,827 --> 00:13:50,568
So the killer must've been
looking for the million bucks.

269
00:13:50,655 --> 00:13:51,743
[Rigsby]
If she had a million bucks,
why would she live

270
00:13:51,831 --> 00:13:53,484
in a dump like this?

271
00:13:53,571 --> 00:13:55,008
[Jane]
Before we get on the freeway
it'd be good

272
00:13:55,095 --> 00:13:56,357
to take a little drive
around the neighborhood.

273
00:13:56,444 --> 00:13:58,489
It'd be good to find
an empty house for sale.

274
00:13:58,576 --> 00:13:59,751
Why would that be good?

275
00:13:59,839 --> 00:14:04,104
Oh...I'm thinking of
moving here.

276
00:14:04,191 --> 00:14:06,802
No secrets, Jane.
No lies. No tricks.

277
00:14:06,889 --> 00:14:09,239
No surprises. The truth.

278
00:14:09,326 --> 00:14:11,328
Oh? Since when
is that the rule?

279
00:14:11,415 --> 00:14:13,200
Since I said so.

280
00:14:13,287 --> 00:14:15,593
- Or else what?
- You're off the unit.

281
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:18,814
That's not leverage.
This is my last case, remember?

282
00:14:18,901 --> 00:14:21,338
The only reason I'm still here
is 'cause I'm worried about

283
00:14:21,425 --> 00:14:24,211
how you guys would cope
without me.

284
00:14:24,298 --> 00:14:27,301
Oh, really? So we're
lost without you, are we?

285
00:14:27,388 --> 00:14:30,565
Well, yeah.
Let's be honest here.

286
00:14:30,652 --> 00:14:31,871
- Let's go.
- Hey!

287
00:14:31,958 --> 00:14:33,263
Drive. Go.

288
00:14:34,177 --> 00:14:35,744
Wait. This..

289
00:14:51,325 --> 00:14:52,456
[sighs]

290
00:14:53,980 --> 00:14:55,155
Whoa!

291
00:14:55,242 --> 00:14:57,722
[tires squeal]

292
00:14:57,809 --> 00:15:01,509
- What'd you say to them?
- Uh, nothing, really.

293
00:15:01,596 --> 00:15:03,250
Total overreaction.

294
00:15:03,337 --> 00:15:04,512
'Yeah.'

295
00:15:07,558 --> 00:15:09,691
We need to find
a house for sale.

296
00:15:11,606 --> 00:15:12,781
Okay.

297
00:15:23,096 --> 00:15:25,402
Ah, there's a sign.

298
00:15:29,754 --> 00:15:30,930
Perfect.

299
00:16:15,235 --> 00:16:17,846
Are you gonna tell me
what that's about?

300
00:16:17,933 --> 00:16:21,197
Uh...wouldn't you
rather it be a surprise?

301
00:16:21,284 --> 00:16:22,416
No.

302
00:16:24,244 --> 00:16:26,724
Oh! Oh, look, look.
Strawberries.

303
00:16:34,819 --> 00:16:36,734
- Strawberries?
- Mm.

304
00:16:39,824 --> 00:16:41,609
See?

305
00:16:41,696 --> 00:16:45,178
- How good is that?
- Mm. Good.

306
00:16:45,265 --> 00:16:47,484
Cho, Rigsby, start
canvassing the neighbors.

307
00:16:47,571 --> 00:16:49,747
Van pelt, come with me.

308
00:16:49,834 --> 00:16:51,271
Uh, I..

309
00:16:51,358 --> 00:16:52,663
Lisbon, uh..

310
00:16:55,579 --> 00:16:59,018
I'm sorry for
what I said to you before.

311
00:16:59,105 --> 00:17:01,846
Can I please
continue working with you?

312
00:17:01,933 --> 00:17:04,066
I thought you were quitting.

313
00:17:04,153 --> 00:17:05,546
You know I didn't mean that.

314
00:17:05,633 --> 00:17:07,113
So the job isworthwhile, is it?

315
00:17:07,200 --> 00:17:09,811
It's not that.
I mean, it's not that at all.

316
00:17:09,898 --> 00:17:11,291
It's just..

317
00:17:16,818 --> 00:17:19,168
I have nothing else to do.

318
00:17:22,258 --> 00:17:25,044
No jokes. From now on,
there have to be boundaries.

319
00:17:25,131 --> 00:17:26,654
Agreed.

320
00:17:26,741 --> 00:17:29,178
I need to know that you can do
your work and be effective

321
00:17:29,265 --> 00:17:31,180
without creating a mess
that I have to clean up.

322
00:17:31,267 --> 00:17:33,139
No mess, I swear.

323
00:17:34,966 --> 00:17:37,534
On that basis,
you can remain with the unit.

324
00:17:37,621 --> 00:17:39,101
Thank you.

325
00:17:43,410 --> 00:17:44,541
Here.

326
00:17:47,196 --> 00:17:48,632
[male #2]
'So how did she die?'

327
00:17:48,719 --> 00:17:51,374
It was a homicide,
but she didn't suffer.

328
00:17:51,461 --> 00:17:53,289
We don't have
any suspects yet.

329
00:17:53,376 --> 00:17:54,943
Where was she?

330
00:17:55,030 --> 00:17:58,251
She was living alone in a small
apartment in Twenty Palms.

331
00:17:58,338 --> 00:18:00,731
I thought she'd be living it up
in Mexico or someplace.

332
00:18:00,818 --> 00:18:03,169
- She was always--
- Stop it.

333
00:18:04,431 --> 00:18:06,694
I need to lie down.

334
00:18:06,781 --> 00:18:09,262
Okay, champ, let's roll.

335
00:18:15,224 --> 00:18:17,270
I'm making some tea.
Would you like a cup?

336
00:18:17,357 --> 00:18:19,402
- No, thank you.
- Oh, oh, I-I'd love a cup.

337
00:18:19,489 --> 00:18:20,621
Thanks.

338
00:18:23,319 --> 00:18:25,278
Let me get you a blanket.
I'll be right back.

339
00:18:29,891 --> 00:18:31,632
Ansel, uh, had cancer.

340
00:18:31,719 --> 00:18:33,721
He's totally better now,
but still weak.

341
00:18:33,808 --> 00:18:35,418
It's in remission?

342
00:18:35,505 --> 00:18:39,857
Gone. Hundred percent.
We were blessed.

343
00:18:39,944 --> 00:18:42,338
Even the doctors were amazed.

344
00:18:44,775 --> 00:18:47,648
Thank you.
Tell me about your mother.

345
00:18:47,735 --> 00:18:50,825
Sure. I'll tell you.

346
00:18:50,912 --> 00:18:52,348
Stealing all that money
and running off

347
00:18:52,435 --> 00:18:54,742
that was just
the icing on the cake.

348
00:18:54,829 --> 00:18:57,527
When Ansel got cancer,
her way of coping?

349
00:18:57,614 --> 00:19:01,575
Painkillers
and a sleazebag lover.

350
00:19:01,662 --> 00:19:04,230
That's mom.

351
00:19:04,317 --> 00:19:06,275
Did you know
her lover's name?

352
00:19:06,362 --> 00:19:08,147
Bodhi Andros.

353
00:19:11,193 --> 00:19:12,847
How we found out?

354
00:19:12,934 --> 00:19:17,721
[scoffs]
Ansel goes to meet
his mom at her yogarobics class.

355
00:19:17,808 --> 00:19:21,899
He finds her banging
the teacher in his office.

356
00:19:21,986 --> 00:19:24,641
- Can you imagine?
- Then what happened?

357
00:19:24,728 --> 00:19:26,600
She did her dance.

358
00:19:26,687 --> 00:19:29,820
She's so ashamed,
she's so sorry

359
00:19:29,907 --> 00:19:31,866
she's changed.

360
00:19:31,953 --> 00:19:33,955
Whatever.

361
00:19:34,042 --> 00:19:36,740
And dad was halfway
to forgiving her.

362
00:19:36,827 --> 00:19:39,221
He can't help it.
He loves her.

363
00:19:39,308 --> 00:19:42,572
But you don't...love her?

364
00:19:42,659 --> 00:19:44,574
She...never had time for me.

365
00:19:44,661 --> 00:19:49,057
So...I could always see through
her charm, you know.

366
00:19:49,144 --> 00:19:52,234
- To the selfish cow beneath--
- 'Kessie!'

367
00:19:52,321 --> 00:19:54,889
Don't talk that way.
She loved you.

368
00:19:57,326 --> 00:19:59,676
Look, she's dead.
Show some respect.

369
00:20:05,378 --> 00:20:09,512
She was a beautiful soul,
full of joy and, and big dreams.

370
00:20:09,599 --> 00:20:12,863
And reality
just kinda beat her down.

371
00:20:12,950 --> 00:20:14,038
But here's the thing.

372
00:20:14,125 --> 00:20:15,736
'It was only because the theft'

373
00:20:15,823 --> 00:20:19,479
was in the news that
the doctors at Cedars of Zion

374
00:20:19,566 --> 00:20:22,133
heard about Ansel
and reached out to help us.

375
00:20:22,221 --> 00:20:25,572
'They took him on for free
and they saved his life.'

376
00:20:25,659 --> 00:20:28,705
Saved his life when everybody
said there was no hope.

377
00:20:28,792 --> 00:20:31,142
'So..'

378
00:20:31,230 --> 00:20:34,624
...you know,
God works in mysterious ways.

379
00:20:34,711 --> 00:20:37,671
Well, you know
God doesn't necessarily--

380
00:20:37,758 --> 00:20:40,674
Thank you very much
for your time.

381
00:20:40,761 --> 00:20:43,372
Yeah. Thank you.

382
00:20:43,459 --> 00:20:44,895
Very nice tea.

383
00:20:47,594 --> 00:20:49,204
Oh, uh, I almost forgot.

384
00:20:49,291 --> 00:20:52,120
Do either of you
know a Miles Thorsen?

385
00:20:52,207 --> 00:20:53,904
- Uh, no, I don't think so.
- No.

386
00:20:53,991 --> 00:20:55,819
Sure?

387
00:20:55,906 --> 00:20:57,778
- Yeah.
- Yes.

388
00:20:57,865 --> 00:20:58,953
Alright.

389
00:21:01,651 --> 00:21:03,131
Ansel.

390
00:21:03,218 --> 00:21:05,046
Hey, Ansel!

391
00:21:07,091 --> 00:21:09,398
You know Miles Thorsen?

392
00:21:09,485 --> 00:21:10,878
No.

393
00:21:10,965 --> 00:21:12,140
Okay.

394
00:21:13,533 --> 00:21:15,230
Bye.

395
00:21:15,317 --> 00:21:16,623
Thank you.

396
00:21:18,755 --> 00:21:21,628
- Who's Miles Thorsen?
- No one in particular.

397
00:21:21,715 --> 00:21:23,804
This is what
I'm talking about.

398
00:21:23,891 --> 00:21:27,198
Oh. No, me, too. Y-you want
no fuss, no mess, right?

399
00:21:27,286 --> 00:21:29,244
I am casting
a wide, invisible net.

400
00:21:29,331 --> 00:21:31,290
The killer won't even know
he's in it until it's too late.

401
00:21:31,377 --> 00:21:33,117
Miles Thorsen
is an invisible net?

402
00:21:33,204 --> 00:21:34,858
Yes, he is.

403
00:21:34,945 --> 00:21:36,469
We got an address
on Bodhi Andros.

404
00:21:36,556 --> 00:21:38,471
Cho, Rigsby,
go and check him out.

405
00:21:38,558 --> 00:21:39,820
Van Pelt,
get on the money trail.

406
00:21:39,907 --> 00:21:41,517
Somebody's got
that million dollars.

407
00:21:41,604 --> 00:21:43,432
Start with the dad
and the daughter.

408
00:21:43,519 --> 00:21:45,434
See if they've been spending
money they shouldn't have.

409
00:21:45,521 --> 00:21:48,611
- They looked clean to me.
- Oh, did they? Okay, great.

410
00:21:48,698 --> 00:21:53,050
- Just let them be.
- I don't...I'll check 'em out.

411
00:21:53,137 --> 00:21:55,444
Okay, come on. Let's get
back to Sacramento, grumpy.

412
00:21:55,531 --> 00:21:58,012
- What's the hurry?
- I have a meeting. With Bosco.

413
00:21:58,099 --> 00:21:59,796
Oh. Good luck.

414
00:21:59,883 --> 00:22:01,798
[sitar music]

415
00:22:01,885 --> 00:22:03,191
Great class, guys.

416
00:22:03,278 --> 00:22:05,454
Hey, and let's use
that focus we found

417
00:22:05,541 --> 00:22:08,936
throughout the week, okay?
Namaste.

418
00:22:09,023 --> 00:22:11,547
Namaste.
Okay, thank you.

419
00:22:11,634 --> 00:22:12,983
[music stops]

420
00:22:15,377 --> 00:22:17,161
- I'm looking for Bodhi Andros.
- Crap!

421
00:22:17,248 --> 00:22:20,426
Yeah, his office is upstairs,
to the right.

422
00:22:20,513 --> 00:22:22,036
[Cho]
Thank you.

423
00:22:22,123 --> 00:22:24,038
Remember that time
you said you'd been in juvie?

424
00:22:24,125 --> 00:22:25,387
Yep.

425
00:22:25,474 --> 00:22:27,171
You never said
what you got sent there for.

426
00:22:27,258 --> 00:22:29,217
It's gang stuff.

427
00:22:29,304 --> 00:22:31,567
You were in a gang?
What was the name of the gang?

428
00:22:31,654 --> 00:22:33,352
Avon Park Playboys.

429
00:22:33,439 --> 00:22:35,136
[chuckles]
Avon Park Playboys?

430
00:22:35,223 --> 00:22:36,877
I didn't choose the name.

431
00:22:36,964 --> 00:22:38,269
[door shuts]

432
00:22:41,621 --> 00:22:44,667
And, uh, what kind of stuff
did the playboys do?

433
00:22:44,754 --> 00:22:47,017
- Nothin' good.
- Like what?

434
00:22:47,104 --> 00:22:48,149
Nothing you need to know about.

435
00:22:48,236 --> 00:22:49,803
Mm, fair enough. Your call.

436
00:22:49,890 --> 00:22:51,935
See, I don't think
partners should have secrets

437
00:22:52,022 --> 00:22:53,284
but your call.

438
00:22:53,372 --> 00:22:54,851
Like you don't
have any secrets.

439
00:22:54,938 --> 00:22:57,071
- What's that supposed to mean?
- You know what I mean.

440
00:22:57,158 --> 00:22:59,900
Hey, you in there.
Police.

441
00:22:59,987 --> 00:23:03,120
CBI. Very slowly, stand up.

442
00:23:03,207 --> 00:23:06,254
[Andros]
'I'm unarmed, man.
I have no weapon.'

443
00:23:06,341 --> 00:23:07,864
Well, that's great.

444
00:23:07,951 --> 00:23:09,953
So come out slowly,
hands on your head

445
00:23:10,040 --> 00:23:13,000
and we won't hurt you, promise.

446
00:23:13,087 --> 00:23:15,959
'Comin' out now.
Don't shoot.'

447
00:23:19,746 --> 00:23:23,140
Morning, sir.
You Bodhi Andros?

448
00:23:23,227 --> 00:23:25,578
- Yes, I am.
- Turn around.

449
00:23:34,891 --> 00:23:37,590
Hey. Sorry, I'm late.
Bad traffic.

450
00:23:37,677 --> 00:23:39,983
- What are you having?
- What areyouhaving?

451
00:23:40,070 --> 00:23:42,246
Well, I'll have
a bottle of water, thanks.

452
00:23:42,333 --> 00:23:45,598
Uh, dog with kraut,
bottle of water.

453
00:23:51,168 --> 00:23:52,605
Thank you.

454
00:23:55,346 --> 00:23:57,784
- Keep it.
- Oh, thank you.

455
00:24:00,177 --> 00:24:01,527
Well, I'm not gonna lie.

456
00:24:01,614 --> 00:24:04,617
I'm not happy
I'm off the Red John case.

457
00:24:04,704 --> 00:24:06,923
You might be right.

458
00:24:07,010 --> 00:24:10,013
Fresh set of eyes is
maybe what's needed.

459
00:24:10,100 --> 00:24:13,103
Here's the thing, Sam,
if I may.

460
00:24:13,190 --> 00:24:14,888
I know this case.

461
00:24:14,975 --> 00:24:16,629
I can be
a very good resource for you.

462
00:24:16,716 --> 00:24:20,633
If we could just come to
some kind of understanding.

463
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:23,287
You want me to, uh,
keep you up to date on the case?

464
00:24:23,374 --> 00:24:24,767
Yeah, sure.

465
00:24:24,854 --> 00:24:26,465
Tell you about fresh leads,
etcetera.

466
00:24:26,552 --> 00:24:28,858
Yeah, let's just keep
an open line of communication.

467
00:24:32,079 --> 00:24:34,081
- Mm.
- Good.

468
00:24:34,168 --> 00:24:38,520
Mm...my wife has me on a diet.

469
00:24:38,607 --> 00:24:40,304
Mm-hmm.

470
00:24:40,391 --> 00:24:42,045
This is like
committin' adultery here.

471
00:24:42,132 --> 00:24:43,307
[chuckles]

472
00:24:46,659 --> 00:24:48,530
Let me communicate this to you.

473
00:24:50,706 --> 00:24:53,187
You're a party entertainer.

474
00:24:53,274 --> 00:24:55,145
A clown.

475
00:24:55,232 --> 00:24:56,712
Fresh leads?

476
00:24:56,799 --> 00:24:58,235
I wouldn't tell you
where the bathroom is

477
00:24:58,322 --> 00:24:59,933
if your ass was on fire.

478
00:25:00,020 --> 00:25:01,151
[chuckles]

479
00:25:02,631 --> 00:25:03,893
Do we have
a good understanding now?

480
00:25:03,980 --> 00:25:05,547
I understand you.

481
00:25:05,634 --> 00:25:09,420
It may take you some time
to understand me.

482
00:25:09,508 --> 00:25:12,815
You're filled with equal parts
self-loathing and self-love.

483
00:25:12,902 --> 00:25:14,600
You're addicted to control.

484
00:25:14,687 --> 00:25:16,427
You're terrified
of confinement.

485
00:25:16,515 --> 00:25:19,343
Who's my favorite Beatle? Huh?

486
00:25:19,430 --> 00:25:21,215
Your wife wanted you
to quit the psychic trade.

487
00:25:21,302 --> 00:25:22,521
She begged you to stop

488
00:25:22,608 --> 00:25:23,957
but you were
making too much money.

489
00:25:24,044 --> 00:25:25,654
You were havin'
too much fun.

490
00:25:25,741 --> 00:25:29,615
You can still hear
her pleading with you.

491
00:25:29,702 --> 00:25:31,051
How am I doin'?

492
00:25:31,138 --> 00:25:32,792
You read the interviews
in my case file.

493
00:25:32,879 --> 00:25:36,317
You can read.
Very impressive.

494
00:25:36,404 --> 00:25:38,537
Look, Patrick

495
00:25:38,624 --> 00:25:41,365
I'm sorry to be
so blunt with you.

496
00:25:41,452 --> 00:25:42,497
Really?

497
00:25:47,589 --> 00:25:49,983
You're not a detective.
You're a victim.

498
00:25:59,079 --> 00:26:01,864
[machines whirring]

499
00:26:05,999 --> 00:26:07,914
You never said,
how was your meeting with Bosco?

500
00:26:08,001 --> 00:26:09,306
What? I can't hear you!

501
00:26:09,393 --> 00:26:11,482
I said,
how did it go with Bosco?

502
00:26:11,570 --> 00:26:12,919
I can't.

503
00:26:17,314 --> 00:26:19,360
How did it go with Bosco?

504
00:26:19,447 --> 00:26:23,059
Oh. Uh...good.
It was, uh, it was very good.

505
00:26:23,146 --> 00:26:25,845
We had a frank exchange
of views.

506
00:26:25,932 --> 00:26:27,020
So he's gonna
keep us in the loop?

507
00:26:27,107 --> 00:26:28,674
- No.
- No?

508
00:26:28,761 --> 00:26:31,154
Wouldn't direct me to the
bathroom if my ass was on fire.

509
00:26:31,241 --> 00:26:32,373
[Lisbon chuckles]

510
00:26:32,460 --> 00:26:33,896
- Think that's funny?
- Yeah.

511
00:26:33,983 --> 00:26:35,855
So that's Mrs. Jaffe over there.

512
00:26:35,942 --> 00:26:37,291
- Thank you.
- Sure.

513
00:26:39,380 --> 00:26:41,034
What are you gonna do?

514
00:26:41,121 --> 00:26:45,168
- Nothing.
- Nothing?

515
00:26:45,255 --> 00:26:48,563
If you sit down by the riverbank
and wait long enough

516
00:26:48,650 --> 00:26:51,610
you will see the bodies
of your enemies float by.

517
00:26:54,482 --> 00:26:55,788
Shall we?

518
00:27:01,402 --> 00:27:02,838
Agent Lisbon, is it?

519
00:27:02,925 --> 00:27:04,666
I'm Rhonda Jaffe,
company chairwoman.

520
00:27:04,753 --> 00:27:06,233
This is my colleague, Mr. Jane.

521
00:27:06,320 --> 00:27:07,800
Hello.

522
00:27:07,887 --> 00:27:09,584
Thank you so much for
taking the time to talk to us.

523
00:27:09,671 --> 00:27:11,325
Oh, not at all.

524
00:27:11,412 --> 00:27:13,719
And you wanted to meet with my
finance director, Greg Humphrey?

525
00:27:13,806 --> 00:27:15,068
- Yes, ma'am.
- Alright.

526
00:27:15,155 --> 00:27:18,071
Well, let's speak in my office.

527
00:27:18,158 --> 00:27:19,550
Did you know
Monica Dunninger?

528
00:27:19,638 --> 00:27:21,727
Oh, yeah.
I know everyone on staff.

529
00:27:21,814 --> 00:27:24,817
So sad. The whole thing
is just so sad.

530
00:27:24,904 --> 00:27:26,253
The poor woman.

531
00:27:26,340 --> 00:27:29,038
More troubled than conniving,
I'm sure.

532
00:27:29,125 --> 00:27:31,650
And you know,
it's not the money.

533
00:27:31,737 --> 00:27:33,956
It's more the loss of trust.

534
00:27:34,043 --> 00:27:36,089
This is very, very
much a family firm.

535
00:27:36,176 --> 00:27:38,526
And my father
always used to say

536
00:27:38,613 --> 00:27:42,138
Jaffe Printing is about values,
not about machinery.

537
00:27:42,225 --> 00:27:44,184
Do you know Miles Thorsen?

538
00:27:44,271 --> 00:27:46,055
Miles Thorsen? No.

539
00:27:46,142 --> 00:27:48,275
- Are you sure?
- Yes.

540
00:27:48,362 --> 00:27:49,929
Seriously, stop it.

541
00:27:50,016 --> 00:27:52,409
Relax.

542
00:27:52,496 --> 00:27:55,238
I saved her life.
She resents me.

543
00:27:55,325 --> 00:27:56,675
Oh, really?

544
00:28:00,374 --> 00:28:02,942
[beep]
Jill?

545
00:28:03,029 --> 00:28:04,160
[beep]

546
00:28:05,335 --> 00:28:06,728
[beep]
'Jill?'

547
00:28:06,815 --> 00:28:08,861
'Jill, where has
Greg Humphrey gone to?'

548
00:28:08,948 --> 00:28:10,558
'He's supposed to be in here.'

549
00:28:10,645 --> 00:28:12,386
[beep]
'Hello?'

550
00:28:12,473 --> 00:28:14,040
[beep]

551
00:28:14,127 --> 00:28:15,389
[male #3]
'Hey.'

552
00:28:15,476 --> 00:28:17,696
Humble apologies.

553
00:28:17,783 --> 00:28:19,741
Small crisis
on the inventory desk.

554
00:28:19,828 --> 00:28:23,092
Solved. Uh, Greg Humphrey.
Good to meet you.

555
00:28:23,179 --> 00:28:24,790
Patrick Jane.

556
00:28:24,877 --> 00:28:26,661
We're investigating
the murder of Monica Dunninger.

557
00:28:26,748 --> 00:28:30,534
Yes. Terrible. Horrible.

558
00:28:30,621 --> 00:28:32,623
You were her direct supervisor,
is that right?

559
00:28:32,711 --> 00:28:36,105
I know what you're gonna say
and yes, yes, it was my fault

560
00:28:36,192 --> 00:28:37,933
that she stole from us.
Mea culpa.

561
00:28:38,020 --> 00:28:39,979
I-I should've known.

562
00:28:40,066 --> 00:28:43,417
Greg is like family practically.
We all make mistakes.

563
00:28:43,504 --> 00:28:46,637
We live, we learn, we move on.

564
00:28:46,725 --> 00:28:48,596
Uh, can you explain
to us how she did it?

565
00:28:48,683 --> 00:28:49,684
In layman's terms?

566
00:28:49,771 --> 00:28:51,381
Uh, been there, done that

567
00:28:51,468 --> 00:28:53,906
with the top men
from the FBI Financial Unit.

568
00:28:53,993 --> 00:28:56,299
Do we really need
to go over it again?

569
00:28:56,386 --> 00:28:59,607
- Yes.
- No problem.

570
00:29:03,829 --> 00:29:05,265
Thank you.

571
00:29:07,093 --> 00:29:08,747
What exactly
was her position here?

572
00:29:08,834 --> 00:29:10,661
Assistant office manager.

573
00:29:10,749 --> 00:29:13,186
She kept
the petty cash accounts.

574
00:29:13,273 --> 00:29:15,449
Coffee and doughnut monitor
essentially.

575
00:29:15,536 --> 00:29:18,800
This was her office.

576
00:29:18,887 --> 00:29:21,194
How did she manage
to steal so much money?

577
00:29:21,281 --> 00:29:23,544
When the accounting and
finance systems were integrated

578
00:29:23,631 --> 00:29:26,677
nobody thought to firewall
the petty cash accounts.

579
00:29:26,765 --> 00:29:30,769
Monica acquired user access
to the transactional sites

580
00:29:30,856 --> 00:29:32,205
and created a false vendor.

581
00:29:32,292 --> 00:29:34,860
I didn't think she had
the brains to be honest.

582
00:29:34,947 --> 00:29:37,514
We don't know
exactly how she did it

583
00:29:37,601 --> 00:29:40,387
because she
fried her hard drive

584
00:29:40,474 --> 00:29:42,389
and she took
her accounts book with her.

585
00:29:42,476 --> 00:29:45,000
Are we about to look at
a whole bunch of numbers?

586
00:29:45,087 --> 00:29:47,046
Probably.

587
00:29:47,133 --> 00:29:48,874
Alright.
I'll wait in the car.

588
00:29:48,961 --> 00:29:50,092
Go.

589
00:29:51,964 --> 00:29:55,445
Do you know a man
named Miles Thorsen?

590
00:29:55,532 --> 00:29:59,058
- No.
- You sure? Think about it.

591
00:29:59,145 --> 00:30:01,495
- I'm sure. Who is he?
- Never mind.

592
00:30:01,582 --> 00:30:03,802
[Lisbon]
'Please continue.'

593
00:30:03,889 --> 00:30:05,542
[cell phone rings]
Would you excuse me?

594
00:30:05,629 --> 00:30:07,283
Hmm.

595
00:30:07,370 --> 00:30:08,807
[Van Pelt]
'Boss, I think
I found something.'

596
00:30:08,894 --> 00:30:10,809
The victim's daughter,
Kessie Dunninger

597
00:30:10,896 --> 00:30:13,463
she handles the family money,
pays the bills.

598
00:30:13,550 --> 00:30:15,117
Five months ago,
she stopped paying the rent

599
00:30:15,204 --> 00:30:17,337
by standing order,
switched to cash.

600
00:30:17,424 --> 00:30:20,427
Same thing with
the utility bills. Paid in cash.

601
00:30:20,514 --> 00:30:22,559
Nice work.
Go and talk to her.

602
00:30:22,646 --> 00:30:24,126
If she doesn't have
a good answer, bring her in.

603
00:30:24,213 --> 00:30:26,520
Will do, boss. Yes!

604
00:30:37,313 --> 00:30:39,838
Why did you hide from us, Bodhi?

605
00:30:39,925 --> 00:30:42,841
Fear, man. Fear.

606
00:30:42,928 --> 00:30:45,408
I've been frightened
for my life ever since I heard.

607
00:30:45,495 --> 00:30:47,889
- Why?
- Who killed her?

608
00:30:47,976 --> 00:30:50,109
Either it was her
goober husband finally snapped

609
00:30:50,196 --> 00:30:51,545
'which puts me next in line'

610
00:30:51,632 --> 00:30:53,112
Or it's whoever she
went into business with

611
00:30:53,199 --> 00:30:54,809
and maybe they think
I know too much.

612
00:30:54,896 --> 00:30:56,593
She was in business
with someone?

613
00:30:56,680 --> 00:30:59,770
Just before she disappeared,
she came by to see me

614
00:30:59,858 --> 00:31:02,077
told me she made
a business deal with someone

615
00:31:02,164 --> 00:31:04,471
that would
make everything alright.

616
00:31:04,558 --> 00:31:06,647
A business deal?
Were those the words she used?

617
00:31:06,734 --> 00:31:08,257
Yep.

618
00:31:08,344 --> 00:31:10,912
She was starting
a new day in her life

619
00:31:10,999 --> 00:31:12,871
and wanted to
make amends and apologize

620
00:31:12,958 --> 00:31:15,308
for being
such a pain in the butt.

621
00:31:15,395 --> 00:31:16,918
- Which she was.
- How so?

622
00:31:17,005 --> 00:31:21,096
She was way too deep
into her family

623
00:31:21,183 --> 00:31:22,489
and to her kids
and all that crap.

624
00:31:22,576 --> 00:31:25,709
- Mm-hmm.
- Loved those kids.

625
00:31:25,796 --> 00:31:28,669
Why do I wanna talk
to her about her sick boy?

626
00:31:28,756 --> 00:31:32,194
- Like I can help?
- You're not a doctor.

627
00:31:32,281 --> 00:31:35,328
Exactly. When we
were caught by that kid

628
00:31:35,415 --> 00:31:38,461
she just...went to pieces,
you know?

629
00:31:38,548 --> 00:31:40,899
And I'm like..

630
00:31:40,986 --> 00:31:45,294
...don't get so down
on yourself.

631
00:31:45,381 --> 00:31:48,819
You gotta loveyoueven if
you're a total screw-up, right?

632
00:31:48,907 --> 00:31:53,781
The greatest...love...of all.

633
00:31:53,868 --> 00:31:56,218
Whitney was right, as usual.

634
00:31:59,874 --> 00:32:01,789
Right.

635
00:32:01,876 --> 00:32:03,573
Do you know a man
named Miles Thorsen?

636
00:32:03,660 --> 00:32:04,835
No. Who's he?

637
00:32:04,923 --> 00:32:06,359
I don't know.

638
00:32:23,506 --> 00:32:25,639
Is that enough sugar?

639
00:32:25,726 --> 00:32:27,206
Yes. Thank you.

640
00:32:28,903 --> 00:32:32,385
So, Kessie,
tell me about the money.

641
00:32:32,472 --> 00:32:36,215
About a month after she ran off,
mom called me.

642
00:32:36,302 --> 00:32:38,391
Said she wanted
to help us out

643
00:32:38,478 --> 00:32:40,045
wanted to know
how Ansel was doing.

644
00:32:40,132 --> 00:32:45,659
She loves us so much,
blah, blah, blah.

645
00:32:45,746 --> 00:32:48,792
I told her to go to hell,
and I hung up.

646
00:32:48,879 --> 00:32:53,188
Two days later, I got a letter
with $5,000 in cash inside.

647
00:32:53,275 --> 00:32:54,668
[Van Pelt]
Any message?

648
00:32:54,755 --> 00:32:57,584
"With love, mom."

649
00:32:57,671 --> 00:32:59,325
That was it.

650
00:32:59,412 --> 00:33:01,892
'What was I supposed to do,
give it to the police?'

651
00:33:01,980 --> 00:33:03,633
Actually, yes.

652
00:33:03,720 --> 00:33:08,682
Well, I didn't.
We really needed the money.

653
00:33:08,769 --> 00:33:10,553
Did you ever tell your dad
about the money?

654
00:33:10,640 --> 00:33:11,815
No.

655
00:33:13,295 --> 00:33:14,775
No.

656
00:33:14,862 --> 00:33:16,646
How often
did she send you money?

657
00:33:16,733 --> 00:33:19,084
$5,000 every month.

658
00:33:19,171 --> 00:33:21,651
Did you ever
write back to her?

659
00:33:21,738 --> 00:33:23,218
No.

660
00:33:23,305 --> 00:33:24,437
I like her for this.

661
00:33:24,524 --> 00:33:27,048
- How so?
- She despises mom.

662
00:33:27,135 --> 00:33:28,745
'Mom's got a million
stolen dollars'

663
00:33:28,832 --> 00:33:30,312
'gets in contact secretly.'

664
00:33:30,399 --> 00:33:32,488
- Do the math.
- Maybe.

665
00:33:32,575 --> 00:33:34,795
No? What's your take?

666
00:33:34,882 --> 00:33:36,623
I'd be guessing.
Could be anybody.

667
00:33:36,710 --> 00:33:40,105
Not anybody. You didn't do it.
I didn't do it.

668
00:33:40,192 --> 00:33:41,584
You're distracted.

669
00:33:41,671 --> 00:33:43,238
You're thinking
about Bosco and Red John.

670
00:33:43,325 --> 00:33:45,240
Focus on the job at hand.

671
00:33:45,327 --> 00:33:48,200
You're glad Bosco has
the Red John case, aren't you?

672
00:33:48,287 --> 00:33:50,680
Bosco is a good agent.
He's as good as they come.

673
00:33:50,767 --> 00:33:52,639
It might not be
such a bad thing

674
00:33:52,726 --> 00:33:54,641
that we're off the case
for a while. We got too close.

675
00:33:54,728 --> 00:33:55,859
Is that what
your shrink tells you?

676
00:33:55,946 --> 00:33:57,296
Everybody tells me that.

677
00:33:57,383 --> 00:33:58,949
But your shrink did
tell you that, didn't he?

678
00:33:59,037 --> 00:34:01,387
Okay, may-maybe you're right.
Maybe, maybe that's the truth.

679
00:34:01,474 --> 00:34:03,345
Maybe we did get a little
too close. I'm not so sure.

680
00:34:03,432 --> 00:34:05,608
- But I'll think about it.
- Liar.

681
00:34:05,695 --> 00:34:08,611
I've got a deposition.
See that box over there?

682
00:34:08,698 --> 00:34:10,744
It's got Monica Dunninger's
possessions in it.

683
00:34:10,831 --> 00:34:12,833
Go through it,
see what you can see.

684
00:34:12,920 --> 00:34:14,313
Yes, ma'am.

685
00:34:19,840 --> 00:34:22,147
Uh, who wants to come
with me out to Twenty Palms

686
00:34:22,234 --> 00:34:23,713
to catch the killer?

687
00:34:23,800 --> 00:34:26,281
Did Lisbon okay this?

688
00:34:26,368 --> 00:34:27,282
Of course.

689
00:34:39,990 --> 00:34:42,036
- What is this place?
- Guess.

690
00:34:42,123 --> 00:34:44,821
- It's Miles Thorsen's house.
- Exactly.

691
00:34:44,908 --> 00:34:48,347
- Who's Miles Thorsen?
- Oh, uh..

692
00:34:48,434 --> 00:34:49,696
I have no idea.

693
00:34:49,783 --> 00:34:51,654
[Rigsby]
'Alright, fine.
Don't tell me.'

694
00:34:59,967 --> 00:35:01,751
- Isn't that your number?
- Uh, yeah, that's my number.

695
00:35:01,838 --> 00:35:03,231
That's in case the killer
gets here before us.

696
00:35:03,318 --> 00:35:05,799
That way he'll call us.

697
00:35:05,886 --> 00:35:08,236
What's that over there?

698
00:35:10,108 --> 00:35:11,457
Hey, we have no warrant.

699
00:35:11,544 --> 00:35:12,719
Don't you hear
someone yelling in there?

700
00:35:12,806 --> 00:35:14,242
No.

701
00:35:14,329 --> 00:35:16,288
- I heard something.
- There's no yelling.

702
00:35:16,375 --> 00:35:19,552
- I have perfect hearing.
- I could swear I heard someone.

703
00:35:26,689 --> 00:35:29,562
[sighs]
Okay, so what are we doing here?

704
00:35:29,649 --> 00:35:34,784
Everybody involved is wondering
who Miles Thorsen is, huh?

705
00:35:34,871 --> 00:35:39,049
The killer will be
doubly curious. Triply, even.

706
00:35:39,137 --> 00:35:41,182
Maybe mysterious Miles Thorsen
has what the killer

707
00:35:41,269 --> 00:35:43,445
is looking for,
what he didn't find.

708
00:35:43,532 --> 00:35:45,447
When they look him up, they'll
learn there's a Miles Thorsen

709
00:35:45,534 --> 00:35:47,667
who lives right around the
corner from Monica. Hey, presto.

710
00:35:47,754 --> 00:35:49,103
This is exactly
the kind of stuff

711
00:35:49,190 --> 00:35:50,539
that boss was talking about.

712
00:35:50,626 --> 00:35:53,325
She didn't okay this at all,
did she?

713
00:35:53,412 --> 00:35:55,022
So shoot me.

714
00:35:55,109 --> 00:35:56,937
'"The Fax Machine Revolution'

715
00:35:57,024 --> 00:36:00,506
'The little box
that's changing our world."'

716
00:36:00,593 --> 00:36:02,943
Why would Monica Dunninger
be reading this book?

717
00:36:03,030 --> 00:36:04,988
You, Cho, shouldn't
enable this kind of crap.

718
00:36:05,075 --> 00:36:06,425
We're not doing anything wrong.
What's the problem?

719
00:36:06,512 --> 00:36:08,601
Uh, burglary, trespassing

720
00:36:08,688 --> 00:36:11,038
willful damage to property.

721
00:36:11,125 --> 00:36:13,475
- The door was open.
- Uh, no, it wasn't.

722
00:36:13,562 --> 00:36:17,000
- Aha! Of course.
- What?

723
00:36:17,087 --> 00:36:18,524
Rigsby, you're right.
You shouldn't be here.

724
00:36:18,611 --> 00:36:21,091
Let us take this.
You go find the nearest library.

725
00:36:21,179 --> 00:36:23,964
Why?

726
00:36:24,051 --> 00:36:26,619
Your punishment for being a
wuss is I'm not gonna tell ya.

727
00:36:26,706 --> 00:36:29,317
- Oh, come on.
- No, I'm not gonna tell you.

728
00:36:29,404 --> 00:36:32,320
Fine. Fine. Whatever.

729
00:36:32,407 --> 00:36:34,888
- Call when you're there.
- 'Oh, ho!'

730
00:36:34,975 --> 00:36:37,020
[door opens and closes]

731
00:36:37,107 --> 00:36:39,327
- So what's the gag?
- Oh, there's no gag.

732
00:36:39,414 --> 00:36:40,459
Solve the case.

733
00:36:40,546 --> 00:36:41,895
I thought you already did that.

734
00:36:41,982 --> 00:36:43,940
I thought that's why
we're waiting here.

735
00:36:44,027 --> 00:36:47,466
Nope. This is, uh..

736
00:36:47,553 --> 00:36:49,250
Well..

737
00:36:49,337 --> 00:36:51,470
This is more of
a fishing expedition.

738
00:36:51,557 --> 00:36:54,908
You know, maybe we get a bite.
Maybe we don't.

739
00:36:54,995 --> 00:36:56,649
That's the fun of it.

740
00:36:56,736 --> 00:37:00,087
We just sit back, relax,
dangle our feet in the water.

741
00:37:01,306 --> 00:37:03,699
[instrumental music]

742
00:37:12,926 --> 00:37:16,016
Now, this guy predicted
that by the year 2000

743
00:37:16,103 --> 00:37:19,367
every American household
will have a fax machine.

744
00:37:20,977 --> 00:37:23,023
Can you believe that?

745
00:37:23,110 --> 00:37:25,373
I had a fax machine.

746
00:37:25,460 --> 00:37:26,679
You?

747
00:37:26,766 --> 00:37:28,028
Yeah.

748
00:37:28,115 --> 00:37:31,510
Wow. Nostradamus
of office equipment.

749
00:37:31,597 --> 00:37:32,859
See, this is why
I don't like fishing.

750
00:37:32,946 --> 00:37:34,513
We're not
gonna catch anything.

751
00:37:34,600 --> 00:37:37,037
We have some very tasty bait.
I'm very confident.

752
00:37:37,124 --> 00:37:39,387
Did you consider that the
killer found the money already?

753
00:37:39,474 --> 00:37:40,780
I don't think it was money
they were looking for.

754
00:37:40,867 --> 00:37:42,912
I don't think
Monica ever had it.

755
00:37:42,999 --> 00:37:44,827
Well, what did she have then?
And who has the money?

756
00:37:44,914 --> 00:37:48,004
[male #4]
'Great bones,
original mid century detail.'

757
00:37:48,091 --> 00:37:52,095
Great new copper piping that--

758
00:37:52,182 --> 00:37:53,445
Hello.

759
00:37:53,532 --> 00:37:56,317
Did you have
a fax machine in 2000?

760
00:37:56,404 --> 00:37:57,971
Um...hi?

761
00:37:58,058 --> 00:37:59,320
No cause for alarm, sir.

762
00:37:59,407 --> 00:38:02,149
We're law enforcement officers.
CBI.

763
00:38:05,326 --> 00:38:07,850
"Kimball Cho." Okay.

764
00:38:07,937 --> 00:38:10,505
What are you doing here,
Kimball? Is there a problem?

765
00:38:10,592 --> 00:38:12,028
It's an ongoing investigation,
sir.

766
00:38:12,115 --> 00:38:14,161
We can't give out any details.

767
00:38:14,248 --> 00:38:16,163
Grisly homicide, though.

768
00:38:18,557 --> 00:38:21,995
Why don't we take a look at that
duplex on Royston? Shall we?

769
00:38:22,082 --> 00:38:23,997
That's a good call.

770
00:38:28,567 --> 00:38:30,482
- Damn it.
- Relax.

771
00:38:30,569 --> 00:38:33,311
- He got my name.
- Well, you spoke the truth.

772
00:38:33,398 --> 00:38:35,095
We are an ongoing investigation.

773
00:38:35,182 --> 00:38:36,966
Yeah, but we're supposed
to be working 100% straight.

774
00:38:37,053 --> 00:38:38,925
Thisis not that.

775
00:38:46,193 --> 00:38:49,239
Oh, I'm, I'm sorry.
Wrong address.

776
00:38:49,327 --> 00:38:50,806
Whoa! Whoa! Rhonda!

777
00:38:51,938 --> 00:38:53,287
Come back.

778
00:38:57,335 --> 00:39:00,555
I'm here out
of curiosity, that's all.

779
00:39:00,642 --> 00:39:02,078
Well, of course.

780
00:39:05,865 --> 00:39:08,302
I've got nothing else
to say without my lawyer.

781
00:39:08,389 --> 00:39:10,957
You don't need
to say anything.

782
00:39:11,044 --> 00:39:13,568
I knew it was you
from the start.

783
00:39:13,655 --> 00:39:15,527
Thanks for sharing.

784
00:39:15,614 --> 00:39:19,052
Well, when I say I-I knew it was
her, I-I mean I-I guessed.

785
00:39:19,139 --> 00:39:21,663
I subconsciously thought
it was her

786
00:39:21,750 --> 00:39:24,884
when I saw a photo of her
holding a large check

787
00:39:24,971 --> 00:39:28,278
outside Cedars of Zion.

788
00:39:28,366 --> 00:39:30,193
It was you that
stole the million dollars

789
00:39:30,280 --> 00:39:32,457
from your own company.

790
00:39:32,544 --> 00:39:34,676
Why would I do that?

791
00:39:34,763 --> 00:39:36,635
Well, degenerate gambling
would be my guess.

792
00:39:36,722 --> 00:39:39,899
'But whatever your problem is,
when threatened with exposure'

793
00:39:39,986 --> 00:39:42,815
you made a deal
with poor Monica.

794
00:39:42,902 --> 00:39:45,992
'If she'd take
the blame and disappear'

795
00:39:46,079 --> 00:39:49,561
you'd get Ansel into the best
cancer program in California

796
00:39:49,648 --> 00:39:52,346
and pay her a monthly wage
to stay gone.

797
00:39:52,433 --> 00:39:54,609
That's absurd.

798
00:39:54,696 --> 00:39:56,132
But then Ansel got cured

799
00:39:56,219 --> 00:39:58,483
and there's no way
Monica was gonna stay gone

800
00:39:58,570 --> 00:40:01,268
not much longer.

801
00:40:01,355 --> 00:40:03,139
Then you had to silence her.

802
00:40:03,226 --> 00:40:05,141
That's pure fiction.

803
00:40:05,228 --> 00:40:07,143
You have no proof at all.

804
00:40:07,230 --> 00:40:10,451
Oh. Oh.
We-we have proof. Wait.

805
00:40:10,538 --> 00:40:12,148
Yeah, where is it?
Here it is. Yep.

806
00:40:12,235 --> 00:40:14,760
[clears throat]
This is gonna be good.

807
00:40:14,847 --> 00:40:15,978
[dialing]

808
00:40:18,416 --> 00:40:21,854
Yep, I'm here.
I know, I know.

809
00:40:21,941 --> 00:40:24,857
"The Fax Machine Revolution"
is a library book

810
00:40:24,944 --> 00:40:27,947
and Monica Dunninger swapped it.

811
00:40:28,034 --> 00:40:30,558
Hold on. I'm checking it.

812
00:40:30,645 --> 00:40:32,168
Alright.

813
00:40:32,255 --> 00:40:34,170
Got it. Wait up.

814
00:40:38,044 --> 00:40:40,438
Looks like an accounts book.

815
00:40:42,918 --> 00:40:44,529
There's a DVD in it.

816
00:40:44,616 --> 00:40:48,054
[female #1]
'This is just to say that..'

817
00:40:48,141 --> 00:40:50,839
'...in case
something happens to me'

818
00:40:50,926 --> 00:40:54,190
that I swear on the Bible

819
00:40:54,277 --> 00:40:56,889
'that everything
in the account book'

820
00:40:56,976 --> 00:40:59,805
'is the truth.'

821
00:40:59,892 --> 00:41:01,241
I know that I've pretty much

822
00:41:01,328 --> 00:41:04,679
lost your love and respect
for good.

823
00:41:04,766 --> 00:41:08,944
'But I hope that
this deal that I've made'

824
00:41:09,031 --> 00:41:12,339
would help Ansel get better

825
00:41:12,426 --> 00:41:16,691
and will make up for some
of the hurt that I've caused.

826
00:41:18,650 --> 00:41:23,829
I love you all so much

827
00:41:23,916 --> 00:41:26,919
especially you, Kessie

828
00:41:27,006 --> 00:41:29,356
'even though you think I don't.'

829
00:41:31,837 --> 00:41:36,102
'I'm just so sorry
for this mess that I've made.'

830
00:41:38,147 --> 00:41:39,758
Please forgive me.

831
00:41:41,847 --> 00:41:42,978
'Goodbye.'

832
00:41:48,201 --> 00:41:50,116
The book
spells out the deal.

833
00:41:50,203 --> 00:41:51,944
Monica took the fall
for her boss

834
00:41:52,031 --> 00:41:54,990
'so that Ansel would have
the best possible treatment.'

835
00:42:05,261 --> 00:42:06,611
Nice work.

836
00:42:13,095 --> 00:42:14,401
[Kessie sobbing]

837
00:42:20,363 --> 00:42:21,539
Oh.

838
00:42:27,153 --> 00:42:30,069
Oh, hi. Sam Bosco here,
CBI Major Crimes Program.

839
00:42:30,156 --> 00:42:31,853
Question.

840
00:42:31,940 --> 00:42:35,814
How do I go about changing
my in-house access passcode?

841
00:42:35,901 --> 00:42:38,817
I got strong reason to
believe it's been compromised.

842
00:42:40,993 --> 00:42:43,386
Yeah, great.

843
00:42:43,473 --> 00:42:45,432
Sure. Sure, I can hold.

844
00:42:52,091 --> 00:42:54,484
[Jane]
Stay tuned for scenes
from our next episode.

845
00:42:57,705 --> 00:42:59,925
[theme music]

